00:45:53.050 --> 00:45:55.055
from a lower level to a higher level.

1034
00:45:55.055 --> 00:45:57.350
Fear of success in other words.

1035
00:45:57.350 --> 00:45:59.710
If I had finished that book,

1036
00:45:59.710 --> 00:46:01.400
no matter how bad it was,

1037
00:46:01.400 --> 00:46:02.610
I would have gone to a higher level.

1038
00:46:02.610 --> 00:46:04.540
I would have been a guy who at least wrote a book

1039
00:46:04.540 --> 00:46:07.320
at age 24 or whatever I was at that time.

1040
00:46:07.320 --> 00:46:10.180
And of course I was completely unconscious of all this,

1041
00:46:10.180 --> 00:46:12.000
no clue what was goin' on.

1042
00:46:12.000 --> 00:46:15.230
All I knew was I was in a state of terror

1043
00:46:15.230 --> 00:46:18.430
and I had to kind of get out of it one way or another.

1044
00:46:18.430 --> 00:46:19.980
If I had been an alcoholic,

1045
00:46:19.980 --> 00:46:23.220
I would have just drunk and wound up in the ditch somewhere.

1046
00:46:23.220 --> 00:46:24.700
Right, right, right (laughs).

1047
00:46:24.700 --> 00:46:27.492
So you just, you implode your whole life

1048
00:46:27.492 --> 00:46:31.180
because that's safer than what might happen if you actually

1049
00:46:31.180 --> 00:46:32.013
finish the book.
Yeah, yeah.

1050
00:46:32.013 --> 00:46:32.890
And I think that speaks

1051
00:46:32.890 --> 00:46:36.380
to just how powerful the resistance is, right?

1052
00:46:36.380 --> 00:46:37.213
Yes.

1053
00:46:37.213 --> 00:46:38.850
And what an unconscious driver

1054
00:46:38.850 --> 00:46:40.780
it can be.
Yes.

1055
00:46:40.780 --> 00:46:44.337
So then you go off across America holding all this.

1056
00:46:44.337 --> 00:46:46.180
You worked in a mental institution,

1057
00:46:46.180 --> 00:46:48.660
you worked on a farm, you did all kinds of stuff,

1058
00:46:48.660 --> 00:46:49.840
right?
Yeah.

1059
00:46:49.840 --> 00:46:50.907
And writing along the way

1060
00:46:50.907 --> 00:46:53.320
or what was the relationship to writing during

1061
00:46:53.320 --> 00:46:54.940
that period of time?
No, I mean,

1062
00:46:54.940 --> 00:46:58.930
it was really a state of running away from it.

1063
00:46:58.930 --> 00:47:02.530
So, I kept, I had my, I lived in my van.

1064
00:47:02.530 --> 00:47:05.720
I had a '65 Chevy van that I went back

1065
00:47:05.720 --> 00:47:08.537
and forth across the country 13 times in,

1066
00:47:08.537 --> 00:47:11.120
and I always had my typewriter with me,

1067
00:47:11.120 --> 00:47:12.200
but I never touched it.

1068
00:47:12.200 --> 00:47:15.760
It was like under, I don't know what, a shirt,

1069
00:47:15.760 --> 00:47:17.470
it was wrapped in something or another,

1070
00:47:17.470 --> 00:47:20.730
but I was just absolutely running away from that.

1071
00:47:20.730 --> 00:47:22.590
But I didn't know it.

1072
00:47:22.590 --> 00:47:24.740
If you had asked me, I wouldn't have said,

1073
00:47:24.740 --> 00:47:26.880
I'd have to come up with some excuse,

1074
00:47:26.880 --> 00:47:29.400
some bogus rationalization.

1075
00:47:29.400 --> 00:47:34.267
But on some deep level, I knew, I knew that...

1076
00:47:35.587 --> 00:47:39.640
I sort of said to myself, "This was a terrible mistake

1077
00:47:39.640 --> 00:47:41.570
I made trying to write in the first place.

1078
00:47:41.570 --> 00:47:42.403
I never should have done it.

1079
00:47:42.403 --> 00:47:44.310
It was really stupid."

1080
00:47:44.310 --> 00:47:46.992
But on some other level I knew this is what,

1081
00:47:46.992 --> 00:47:48.610
I gotta come back to this somehow.

1082
00:47:48.610 --> 00:47:53.190
I gotta slay this dragon somehow or it's gonna kill me.

1083
00:47:53.190 --> 00:47:54.628
Right, well, otherwise you woulda just gone back

1084
00:47:54.628 --> 00:47:56.847
and worked at the ad agency, right?

1085
00:47:56.847 --> 00:47:58.240
Why did you just,
Yeah.

1086
00:47:58.240 --> 00:48:00.075
light off onto the terrain,

1087
00:48:00.075 --> 00:48:03.537
in this sort of Mark Twain kinda way (laughs)?

1088
00:48:04.680 --> 00:48:07.390
Well, we're getting into some deep stuff here, Rich,

1089
00:48:07.390 --> 00:48:11.510
but what happened was maybe you can relate

1090
00:48:11.510 --> 00:48:13.303
to this from your own experiences.

1091
00:48:16.370 --> 00:48:18.270
I remember I had an interview.

1092
00:48:18.270 --> 00:48:19.550
I did try to go back to an ad agency.

1093
00:48:19.550 --> 00:48:23.270
I had an interview with a guy that I had worked with before

1094
00:48:23.270 --> 00:48:25.140
who would become a boss, blah, blah, blah.

1095
00:48:25.140 --> 00:48:27.940
And when I went in to that interview,

1096
00:48:27.940 --> 00:48:32.940
I must have stunk with such loserdom

1097
00:48:33.270 --> 00:48:36.440
or whatever it was that I was like toxic

1098
00:48:36.440 --> 00:48:40.230
and I could see in people's eyes that they saw this on me.

1099
00:48:40.230 --> 00:48:43.580
And it was like, "Get this guy outta here," whatever it is.

1100
00:48:43.580 --> 00:48:45.540
And I had a couple of more things like that.

1101
00:48:45.540 --> 00:48:47.069
Didn't take many to do that

1102
00:48:47.069 --> 00:48:50.560
and I just realized somehow I had fallen out

1103
00:48:50.560 --> 00:48:52.540
of the bottom of the middle-class.

1104
00:48:52.540 --> 00:48:54.013
You know how, if you've been to college

1105
00:48:54.013 --> 00:48:57.210
and you can speak with polysyllabic words

1106
00:48:57.210 --> 00:48:58.497
you would go into a room and people,

1107
00:48:58.497 --> 00:49:00.933
"Oh, this is one of us," et cetera.

1108
00:49:01.780 --> 00:49:04.458
For whatever reason, I had kinda fallen through

1109
00:49:04.458 --> 00:49:08.624
the bottom of it and the reason that I worked

1110
00:49:08.624 --> 00:49:09.778
a bunch of blue collar jobs

1111
00:49:09.778 --> 00:49:12.330
is not like it was any plan of mine.

1112
00:49:12.330 --> 00:49:15.540
It was like, those were the only jobs I could get.

1113
00:49:15.540 --> 00:49:17.301
Hm, interesting.

1114
00:49:17.301 --> 00:49:21.820
So yeah, I just kinda fell through the floor.

1115
00:49:21.820 --> 00:49:24.040
The floor opened up and I went through the trap door.

1116
00:49:24.040 --> 00:49:24.873
Went through the bottom.

1117
00:49:24.873 --> 00:49:29.267
And so you're kinda goin' from gig to gig,

1118
00:49:29.267 --> 00:49:32.100
got the typewriter, refuse to get rid of it

1119
00:49:32.100 --> 00:49:33.551
even though you're not using it.

1120
00:49:33.551 --> 00:49:38.551
So, walk me up to the point where the pain of this reality

1121
00:49:39.470 --> 00:49:42.240
that you were experiencing just became too much

1122
00:49:42.240 --> 00:49:44.360
and you kind of have this tipping point.

1123
00:49:44.360 --> 00:49:46.520
Actually, I write about this in "The War of Art",

1124
00:49:46.520 --> 00:49:48.320
but let me, I'll tell you one other little story

1125
00:49:48.320 --> 00:49:49.640
before we get to that.

1126
00:49:49.640 --> 00:49:51.027
After, I don't know how many years it was,

1127
00:49:51.027 --> 00:49:52.840
it was only maybe three or four years.

1128
00:49:52.840 --> 00:49:55.110
It wasn't like an endless amount of time

1129
00:49:55.110 --> 00:49:56.713
of going around the country,

1130
00:49:57.642 --> 00:50:02.385
I finally, I just kinda gave up on that whole life.

1131
00:50:02.385 --> 00:50:06.407
I know I'm goin' on here.
No, no, no, go.

1132
00:50:06.407 --> 00:50:09.796
But one of the concepts I had in my mind came

1133
00:50:09.796 --> 00:50:12.690
from Jack Kerouac's book, "On the Road,"

1134
00:50:12.690 --> 00:50:14.240
and from the whole, this was kinda

1135
00:50:14.240 --> 00:50:16.270
the '60s and the early '70s.

1136
00:50:16.270 --> 00:50:19.270
And I felt like if I could get myself

1137
00:50:19.270 --> 00:50:20.973
to this mystical place,

1138
00:50:21.890 --> 00:50:23.940
I could be someone that could walk

1139
00:50:23.940 --> 00:50:26.980
in anywhere to any situation, I could relate to it,

1140
00:50:26.980 --> 00:50:29.880
I could find work, I could make friends, da da da da,

1141
00:50:29.880 --> 00:50:30.840
if I could just get

1142
00:50:30.840 --> 00:50:33.230
to that place instead of being so stuck

1143
00:50:33.230 --> 00:50:35.900
in my own head and so afraid and all that sorta stuff.

1144
00:50:35.900 --> 00:50:37.850
And that was kinda my ideal.

1145
00:50:37.850 --> 00:50:40.520
And at some point, I was actually in San Francisco,

1146
00:50:40.520 --> 00:50:45.120
and I was down to getting a job as a driving instructor.

1147
00:50:45.120 --> 00:50:47.092
And for me, that was like the lowest

1148
00:50:47.092 --> 00:50:49.040
'cause I had driven trucks.

1149
00:50:49.040 --> 00:50:51.550
I had done, somehow driving was my thing

1150
00:50:51.550 --> 00:50:53.270
for whatever it was.

1151
00:50:53.270 --> 00:50:54.170
And I just gave up.

1152
00:50:54.170 --> 00:50:55.500
I said, "I can't do this.

1153
00:50:55.500 --> 00:50:56.762
I'm not Jack Kerouac.

1154
00:50:56.762 --> 00:50:58.887
I can't live this life on the road.

1155
00:50:58.887 --> 00:51:01.230
I'm just, I give up.

1156
00:51:01.230 --> 00:51:02.260
I'm goin' home.

1157
00:51:02.260 --> 00:51:03.830
I'm goin' back to New York.

1158
00:51:03.830 --> 00:51:06.340
I'm gonna take the shittiest job I can find

1159
00:51:06.340 --> 00:51:08.620
and I'm gonna try to little by little kind of work

1160
00:51:08.620 --> 00:51:11.750
my way back into the middle-class however I could."

1161
00:51:11.750 --> 00:51:16.750
And I was driving across the country back to New York,

1162
00:51:17.660 --> 00:51:21.380
taking the Southern route and I met this couple,

1163
00:51:21.380 --> 00:51:25.130
a cowboy and his wife who had just gotten married

1164
00:51:25.130 --> 00:51:26.520
and they had all their possessions

1165
00:51:26.520 --> 00:51:28.370
in a paper bag between 'em.

1166
00:51:28.370 --> 00:51:30.800
And the short version of the story was

1167
00:51:30.800 --> 00:51:33.100
we kinda became friends over a couple of days

1168
00:51:33.100 --> 00:51:37.340
and he said, "I'm goin' to be workin' on my uncle's ranch.

1169
00:51:37.340 --> 00:51:39.130
Why don't you come with me?"

1170
00:51:39.130 --> 00:51:40.530
And I said, "I can't ride a horse.

1171
00:51:40.530 --> 00:51:41.567
I don't want anything about that."

1172
00:51:41.567 --> 00:51:43.140
"I'll teach ya, no problem."

1173
00:51:43.140 --> 00:51:45.450
And I thought to myself, "Hm, a cowboy.

1174
00:51:45.450 --> 00:51:46.410
I've never been a cowboy.

1175
00:51:46.410 --> 00:51:47.243
Maybe I should try..."

1176
00:51:47.243 --> 00:51:50.349
And I just said to myself, "I can't do this anymore.

1177
00:51:50.349 --> 00:51:53.010
I can't do this anymore.

1178
00:51:53.010 --> 00:51:55.482
I've just gotta go home."

1179
00:51:55.482 --> 00:52:00.482
So, I did go home and to keep blathering along here, Rich,

1180
00:52:00.800 --> 00:52:03.810
there's a chapter in "The War of Art"

1181
00:52:03.810 --> 00:52:05.070
where I talk about this.

1182
00:52:05.070 --> 00:52:08.690
I got back to New York, I got a job drivin' a cab

1183
00:52:08.690 --> 00:52:10.508
and I was in a job tending bar

1184
00:52:10.508 --> 00:52:13.030
and I had a little sublet apartment.

1185
00:52:13.030 --> 00:52:17.853
And one night I was just, I was sitting there and I went

1186
00:52:18.790 --> 00:52:21.342
into that sort of what I imagined an alcoholic goes

1187
00:52:21.342 --> 00:52:23.395
into when they really need a drink.

1188
00:52:23.395 --> 00:52:25.797
And I just thought, I thought,

1189
00:52:25.797 --> 00:52:26.650
"Who could I call?

1190
00:52:26.650 --> 00:52:27.509
Are there any women I could call

1191
00:52:27.509 --> 00:52:29.380
that I could kind of go over to their place

1192
00:52:29.380 --> 00:52:30.722
or is there somebody I could,"

1193
00:52:30.722 --> 00:52:34.870
just, and I said to myself, "I just can't do this anymore,"

1194
00:52:34.870 --> 00:52:37.310
and I pulled out that typewriter.

1195
00:52:37.310 --> 00:52:39.460
And like I say, this is in "The War of Art,"

1196
00:52:39.460 --> 00:52:42.890
and I sat down for like two hours, just typing some story.

1197
00:52:42.890 --> 00:52:43.723
I don't know what it was.

1198
00:52:43.723 --> 00:52:44.630
Whatever it was I threw it away.

1199
00:52:44.630 --> 00:52:45.870
It was terrible, right?

1200
00:52:45.870 --> 00:52:50.260
And I went into wash, there was some dishes in the sink.

1201
00:52:50.260 --> 00:52:52.430
And as I started washing the dishes,

1202
00:52:52.430 --> 00:52:55.960
I realized that I was whistling and I sort of had

1203
00:52:55.960 --> 00:52:59.210
this sense that like I was okay.

1204
00:52:59.210 --> 00:53:03.090
Then I thought, "Oh, I can sit down at the typewriter.

1205
00:53:03.090 --> 00:53:06.830
I'm like a million miles from doing anything good,

1206
00:53:06.830 --> 00:53:10.030
but finally I can actually sit and try,"

1207
00:53:10.030 --> 00:53:12.500
and like this great weight went

1208
00:53:12.500 --> 00:53:14.497
off my shoulders at that point and I thought,

1209
00:53:14.497 --> 00:53:16.230
"It may take me another 30 years,"

1210
00:53:16.230 --> 00:53:19.640
which it did to do anything decent

1211
00:53:19.640 --> 00:53:22.950
but at least I can do it now and I don't know why

1212
00:53:22.950 --> 00:53:24.660
I could when I couldn't before.

1213
00:53:24.660 --> 00:53:27.630
Maybe because I'd tried everything else

1214
00:53:27.630 --> 00:53:29.037
under the sun.
Right, right.

1215
00:53:29.037 --> 00:53:31.410
And I just knew I couldn't try that anymore.

1216
00:53:31.410 --> 00:53:32.243
Right.

1217
00:53:32.243 --> 00:53:33.940
What was your self-awareness

1218
00:53:33.940 --> 00:53:36.270
around that moment at the time?

1219
00:53:36.270 --> 00:53:38.130
Nothing more than what I just told you.

1220
00:53:38.130 --> 00:53:43.130
I just felt like, I now can sit at a typewriter

1221
00:53:43.620 --> 00:53:47.516
and try to work and I'm gonna be okay.

1222
00:53:47.516 --> 00:53:49.880
I'm not gonna fall off the end of the earth.

1223
00:53:49.880 --> 00:53:51.710
I'm not gonna go insane.

1224
00:53:51.710 --> 00:53:52.610
I'm gonna be okay.

1225
00:53:52.610 --> 00:53:53.540
Right.

1226
00:53:53.540 --> 00:53:56.140
To extend the alcoholic analogy,

1227
00:53:56.140 --> 00:53:57.500
I mean, there's so many similarities.

1228
00:53:57.500 --> 00:53:59.600
It's sorta like the alcoholic trying everything

1229
00:53:59.600 --> 00:54:01.824
before finally just giving up

1230
00:54:01.824 --> 00:54:05.497
and raising their hand and saying, "I need help," right?

1231
00:54:05.497 --> 00:54:07.692
"I'm gonna drink only after five o'clock.

1232
00:54:07.692 --> 00:54:09.180
I'm only gonna drink beer."

1233
00:54:09.180 --> 00:54:12.340
You have to do all of that and exhaust

1234
00:54:12.340 --> 00:54:14.645
all of it before you're so depleted

1235
00:54:14.645 --> 00:54:18.370
and ready for something new, like that change.

1236
00:54:18.370 --> 00:54:22.230
And in your situation, taking all of these different jobs,

1237
00:54:22.230 --> 00:54:24.078
it would be one thing if you were a truck driver,

1238
00:54:24.078 --> 00:54:28.125
a cowboy, whatever, if that was part of the plan

1239
00:54:28.125 --> 00:54:31.486
of collecting amazing experiences to write about

1240
00:54:31.486 --> 00:54:33.360
but that wasn't what you were doing.

1241
00:54:33.360 --> 00:54:34.351
These were just,
No, absolutely not.

1242
00:54:34.351 --> 00:54:36.130
you were just running away from your life,

1243
00:54:36.130 --> 00:54:36.963
essentially.
Yeah.

1244
00:54:36.963 --> 00:54:38.107
Now you must look back and think,

1245
00:54:38.107 --> 00:54:39.693
"Oh, I've got all these rich experiences

1246
00:54:39.693 --> 00:54:42.010
that I can tap into.

1247
00:54:42.010 --> 00:54:44.490
I do think those experiences were important

1248
00:54:44.490 --> 00:54:46.070
even though I haven't actually ever tapped

1249
00:54:46.070 --> 00:54:47.900
into those specific ones.

1250
00:54:47.900 --> 00:54:50.290
But if I look, I'm a believer in the muse,

1251
00:54:50.290 --> 00:54:52.180
I believe there's a goddess up there.

1252
00:54:52.180 --> 00:54:53.840
And if I think of the muse

1253
00:54:53.840 --> 00:54:56.550
like watching over me at this time,

1254
00:54:56.550 --> 00:54:57.530
she would say, "Oh, look at him

1255
00:54:57.530 --> 00:54:59.071
go down this blind alley."

1256
00:54:59.071 --> 00:54:59.904
Right, right.

1257
00:54:59.904 --> 00:55:01.320
"Look at him go down that blind alley."

1258
00:55:01.320 --> 00:55:03.070
And then finally, when I come back and I sit down

1259
00:55:03.070 --> 00:55:06.487
at the typewriter, I think she finally perks up and goes,

1260
00:55:06.487 --> 00:55:09.800
"Ah, the son of a bitch is finally sitting down

1261
00:55:09.800 --> 00:55:11.110
and doin' what I've been waiting

1262
00:55:11.110 --> 00:55:13.617
for him to do for all this time."

1263
00:55:13.617 --> 00:55:16.937
And now from the goddesses point of view she would say,

1264
00:55:16.937 --> 00:55:18.157
"Okay, I'm gonna give him something.

1265
00:55:18.157 --> 00:55:20.261
I'm gonna give him an idea.

1266
00:55:20.261 --> 00:55:22.400
I've been holding back.

1267
00:55:22.400 --> 00:55:24.100
Now, I'm gonna help him a little bit."

1268
00:55:24.100 --> 00:55:24.933
Right.

1269
00:55:24.933 --> 00:55:27.850
I mean, there is a divinity in being that broken and-

1270
00:55:27.850 --> 00:55:28.683
Definitely.

1271
00:55:28.683 --> 00:55:32.340
And something to be revered about hitting bottom.

1272
00:55:32.340 --> 00:55:33.870
And it's something that I think about a lot

1273
00:55:33.870 --> 00:55:36.460
'cause I'm involved in the recovery community.

1274
00:55:36.460 --> 00:55:40.310
It's like, do you step in and try to divert somebody

1275
00:55:40.310 --> 00:55:43.860
from meeting that kind of predicament?

1276
00:55:43.860 --> 00:55:45.620
Or is that exactly what they need?

1277
00:55:45.620 --> 00:55:47.707
I mean, I know in my own case, I've had a couple bottoms

1278
00:55:47.707 --> 00:55:50.900
and they were transformational and I look back

1279
00:55:50.900 --> 00:55:52.680
on them with great gratitude.

1280
00:55:52.680 --> 00:55:54.320
There was so much pain

1281
00:55:54.320 --> 00:55:57.320
that I don't wanna ever experience that again.

1282
00:55:57.320 --> 00:55:58.670
But they were the catalyst

1283
00:55:58.670 --> 00:56:02.980
for the greatest growth experiences that I've ever had.

1284
00:56:02.980 --> 00:56:05.280
So, there is something to be said

1285
00:56:05.280 --> 00:56:08.600
for standing back and being the observer,

1286
00:56:08.600 --> 00:56:09.480
as opposed to-
Yeah, instead

1287
00:56:09.480 --> 00:56:10.520
of letting somebody be in a box.

1288
00:56:10.520 --> 00:56:11.353
Yeah, yeah.

1289
00:56:11.353 --> 00:56:12.250
Which is one of the things I really loved

1290
00:56:12.250 --> 00:56:15.024
about "Finding Ultra" that that really came through.

1291
00:56:15.024 --> 00:56:17.951
That was absolutely, as I said,

1292
00:56:17.951 --> 00:56:20.100
that's for real, that's the real thing.

1293
00:56:20.100 --> 00:56:25.010
Yeah, I still wish it hadn't happened, but yeah.

1294
00:56:25.010 --> 00:56:30.010
I mean, you can't shortcut somebody's growth trajectory

1295
00:56:32.760 --> 00:56:35.117
and that applies to the pursuit

1296
00:56:35.117 --> 00:56:38.870
of art and creativity as well, right?

1297
00:56:38.870 --> 00:56:41.023
Like something that I really appreciate

1298
00:56:41.023 --> 00:56:43.640
about what you talk about and write about

1299
00:56:43.640 --> 00:56:48.190
is the fact that there is no hack here, right?

1300
00:56:48.190 --> 00:56:50.710
Like we're in this culture where everybody's looking

1301
00:56:50.710 --> 00:56:51.543
for the shortcut

1302
00:56:51.543 --> 00:56:52.745
and how can I,
Yeah, exactly.

1303
00:56:52.745 --> 00:56:54.980
eradicate all the pain and just

1304
00:56:54.980 --> 00:56:56.310
get right to the good stuff?

1305
00:56:56.310 --> 00:56:58.210
And it just doesn't work that way.

1306
00:56:58.210 --> 00:57:02.560
And rather than fighting that, just embrace that

1307
00:57:02.560 --> 00:57:07.400
because that's part of the toil and the joy that contributes

1308
00:57:07.400 --> 00:57:09.439
to what it is that you're trying to express.

1309
00:57:09.439 --> 00:57:12.120
It's so hard to do though, to keep doin' that stuff.

1310
00:57:12.120 --> 00:57:14.660
But I remember one moment I was workin'

1311
00:57:14.660 --> 00:57:17.480
in the oil fields in Louisiana,

1312
00:57:17.480 --> 00:57:20.280
and I had this friend that,

1313
00:57:20.280 --> 00:57:24.330
we lived in this little bunkhouse together, and his brother,

1314
00:57:24.330 --> 00:57:26.410
he had an older brother who had also gone

1315
00:57:26.410 --> 00:57:28.270
through one of these odysseys, right?

1316
00:57:28.270 --> 00:57:29.960
And he was tellin' me that,

1317
00:57:29.960 --> 00:57:31.000
my friend was tellin' me one day

1318
00:57:31.000 --> 00:57:32.850
that his brother had finally like gotten out of it.

1319
00:57:32.850 --> 00:57:34.430
He got married, everything was good.

1320
00:57:34.430 --> 00:57:36.187
And I said, "Well, how old is he now?"

1321
00:57:36.187 --> 00:57:37.020
And I forgot what the age was,

1322
00:57:37.020 --> 00:57:39.830
but it was like 18 years old.

1323
00:57:39.830 --> 00:57:40.730
God, our age.

1324
00:57:40.730 --> 00:57:42.770
I said, "Oh, shit, you mean we gotta

1325
00:57:42.770 --> 00:57:44.600
go through this for another 18 years?"

1326
00:57:44.600 --> 00:57:47.957
But, I mean, as an entertainment lawyer

1327
00:57:47.957 --> 00:57:49.242
being in the movie business

1328
00:57:49.242 --> 00:57:50.750
and knowing all that you know about

1329
00:57:50.750 --> 00:57:54.600
the all is lost moment which is the,

1330
00:57:54.600 --> 00:57:57.980
like in any story you always gotta sort of take that,

1331
00:57:57.980 --> 00:57:59.260
your character, the protagonist,

1332
00:57:59.260 --> 00:58:00.093
to that moment
Right, you gotta get

1333
00:58:00.093 --> 00:58:01.740
where they hit bottom,
to the bottom

1334
00:58:01.740 --> 00:58:02.610
right?
of the second act.

1335
00:58:02.610 --> 00:58:06.356
And at that point, then they can start to come up

1336
00:58:06.356 --> 00:58:10.790
and it's a cliche of movies and of stories,

1337
00:58:10.790 --> 00:58:12.710
but it's life, it's real life.

1338
00:58:12.710 --> 00:58:13.690
Right.

1339
00:58:13.690 --> 00:58:15.683
The hero's journey.
Yeah.

1340
00:58:15.683 --> 00:58:18.413
That archetype is so powerful.

1341
00:58:18.413 --> 00:58:21.330
It obviously is front and center

1342
00:58:21.330 --> 00:58:24.990
